1
00:00:00,641 --> 00:00:02,273
One hunter, or a colony?

2
00:00:03,065 --> 00:00:05,098
Trace that pale line through the water.

3
00:00:05,929 --> 00:00:08,061
Where would you say one animal ends and

4
00:00:08,073 --> 00:00:09,124
the next begins?

5
00:00:10,645 --> 00:00:13,657
Look at this photograph from a dive off Puerto Rico.

6
00:00:14,507 --> 00:00:15,498
Start at the tip.

7
00:00:16,288 --> 00:00:18,820
Follow the row of clear bells down to the bend.

8
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:22,031
See how the pattern changes there?

9
00:00:22,902 --> 00:00:24,933
If these were just links in a chain,

10
00:00:25,203 --> 00:00:27,735
you'd expect the next link to do the same thing.

11
00:00:29,126 --> 00:00:30,337
Here's the surprise.

12
00:00:31,189 --> 00:00:33,422
A siphonophore grows as a colony.

13
00:00:34,253 --> 00:00:36,927
The repeating parts are animals called zooids,

14
00:00:37,157 --> 00:00:38,509
and they stay attached.

15
00:00:39,770 --> 00:00:41,401
Watch a swimming bell squeeze.

16
00:00:42,252 --> 00:00:44,022
Water shoots out of the opening.

17
00:00:44,813 --> 00:00:47,202
The push moves the bell and carries its

18
00:00:47,214 --> 00:00:48,545
neighbors along with it.

19
00:00:49,816 --> 00:00:53,210
Farther down the stem, a tentacle trails in the water.

20
00:00:54,081 --> 00:00:55,994
Its stinging cells can catch prey.

21
00:00:56,845 --> 00:00:59,819
A feeding zooid has the mouth that takes the meal in.

22
00:01:01,151 --> 00:01:02,803
So where does one hunter end?

23
00:01:03,675 --> 00:01:06,569
You can pick out the individual swimmers and feeders.

24
00:01:07,401 --> 00:01:09,755
But follow a meal or a single stroke,

25
00:01:10,005 --> 00:01:12,319
and you're watching the whole colony at work.

26
00:01:14,103 --> 00:01:16,273
Take that curve in the photograph and

27
00:01:16,285 --> 00:01:17,637
straighten it in your mind.

28
00:01:18,488 --> 00:01:21,140
You could travel from the clear end to the pink end

29
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:23,562
without ever crossing open water.

30
00:01:24,433 --> 00:01:25,844
That connection matters.

31
00:01:26,695 --> 00:01:28,867
A school of fish can make one shape, too.

32
00:01:29,698 --> 00:01:32,549
When the school splits, each fish takes its own

33
00:01:32,561 --> 00:01:34,272
engine and its own mouth with it.

34
00:01:35,523 --> 00:01:37,314
A siphonophore starts small,

35
00:01:37,504 --> 00:01:39,315
from a single fertilized egg.

36
00:01:40,185 --> 00:01:43,134
As it grows, new individuals bud from tissue

37
00:01:43,146 --> 00:01:44,257
that's already there.

38
00:01:45,107 --> 00:01:46,378
They keep the connection.

39
00:01:47,228 --> 00:01:50,520
Imagine drawing its family tree on transparent paper,

40
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:53,361
then laying the drawing over the living animal.

41
00:01:54,231 --> 00:01:56,620
In places, the family tree would still

42
00:01:56,632 --> 00:01:57,923
be physically joined.

43
00:01:59,133 --> 00:02:01,943
In the swimming region, one of those individuals

44
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:02,985
becomes a bell.

45
00:02:03,836 --> 00:02:05,885
It can change shape, push water,

46
00:02:05,897 --> 00:02:07,287
and help move its neighbors.

47
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:10,627
Look for a mouth on that swimmer and you'll be

48
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:12,030
looking in the wrong place.

49
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:13,831
Farther along,

50
00:02:14,161 --> 00:02:16,510
a feeding individual can take in a meal that a

51
00:02:16,522 --> 00:02:17,753
swimmer never could.

52
00:02:19,083 --> 00:02:20,734
Now try to steer the colony.

53
00:02:21,544 --> 00:02:23,633
You have several jets pointing away from

54
00:02:23,645 --> 00:02:24,696
a slender body.

55
00:02:25,526 --> 00:02:27,555
You want to turn left without rolling

56
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:28,678
the whole thing over.

57
00:02:29,488 --> 00:02:31,699
Would you fire the strongest bell first?

58
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:35,759
A study of a siphonophore called Nanomia found a

59
00:02:35,771 --> 00:02:38,903
surprisingly good reason to start with the small ones.

60
00:02:40,193 --> 00:02:42,584
This image comes from the researchers who

61
00:02:42,596 --> 00:02:44,948
investigated how Nanomia swims.

62
00:02:45,839 --> 00:02:47,490
Look along the swimming region.

63
00:02:48,301 --> 00:02:50,313
The youngest bells sit near the tip.

64
00:02:51,124 --> 00:02:53,355
Farther back are larger, older bells.

65
00:02:54,347 --> 00:02:57,439
Age changes more than how much water a bell can expel.

66
00:02:58,350 --> 00:03:00,862
It changes where the push lands on the colony.

67
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:04,504
Picture a door that's standing a little open.

68
00:03:05,335 --> 00:03:06,805
Press close to its hinge,

69
00:03:07,115 --> 00:03:09,346
then press just as gently at the handle.

70
00:03:10,197 --> 00:03:12,328
Your hand supplies the same push,

71
00:03:12,658 --> 00:03:15,409
but the second push turns the door more easily.

72
00:03:16,259 --> 00:03:18,250
In the water there's no fixed hinge.

73
00:03:19,101 --> 00:03:22,210
The useful part of that comparison is the distance

74
00:03:22,222 --> 00:03:24,293
from the point you're turning around.

75
00:03:25,583 --> 00:03:27,975
The young bells near the front have leverage.

76
00:03:28,866 --> 00:03:31,478
Even a modest jet there can turn Nanomia.

77
00:03:32,429 --> 00:03:35,302
Older bells closer to the base of the swimming region

78
00:03:35,572 --> 00:03:37,664
contribute more to forward thrust.

79
00:03:38,574 --> 00:03:40,306
Now watch the diagram again.

80
00:03:41,177 --> 00:03:43,168
First the nose changes direction.

81
00:03:44,019 --> 00:03:46,851
Then the larger bells help carry the feeding region

82
00:03:47,002 --> 00:03:48,073
onto the new heading.

83
00:03:49,243 --> 00:03:51,795
There's another trick inside a single stroke.

84
00:03:52,645 --> 00:03:55,355
Researchers filmed several species swimming and

85
00:03:55,367 --> 00:03:58,078
compared neighboring bells frame by frame.

86
00:03:59,009 --> 00:04:01,319
On one side, the pulses could follow one

87
00:04:01,331 --> 00:04:02,461
another in a wave.

88
00:04:03,312 --> 00:04:05,821
Across the two sides, they didn't see the same

89
00:04:05,833 --> 00:04:06,964
lockstep timing.

90
00:04:07,815 --> 00:04:10,744
A colony can travel together without every swimmer

91
00:04:10,756 --> 00:04:13,088
squeezing at the very same instant.

92
00:04:14,318 --> 00:04:16,989
Keep your eye on the tentacles as the swimmer

93
00:04:17,001 --> 00:04:18,052
leaves the frame.

94
00:04:18,903 --> 00:04:21,594
They trail behind, in water the colony has

95
00:04:21,606 --> 00:04:22,657
just passed through.

96
00:04:23,568 --> 00:04:26,579
Moving a feeding apparatus into position is

97
00:04:26,591 --> 00:04:27,902
only half the problem.

98
00:04:28,853 --> 00:04:31,524
Once it's there, how does something so delicate bring

99
00:04:31,536 --> 00:04:33,167
a fast fish within reach?

100
00:04:34,378 --> 00:04:37,049
Picture the view from a small fish in midwater.

101
00:04:37,859 --> 00:04:39,649
The expedition lamps go out.

102
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:43,130
The great sweep of transparent tissue disappears.

103
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:46,549
What remains visible might be a moving point of

104
00:04:46,561 --> 00:04:48,589
light, with nothing around it to

105
00:04:48,601 --> 00:04:49,972
reveal what's holding it.

106
00:04:50,822 --> 00:04:52,652
For one deep Pacific hunter,

107
00:04:52,922 --> 00:04:54,793
that difference is the opportunity.

108
00:04:56,023 --> 00:04:57,734
Meet the angler siphonophore,

109
00:04:57,904 --> 00:04:59,034
Erenna sirena.

110
00:04:59,845 --> 00:05:02,954
The clear animal photographed off Puerto Rico at the

111
00:05:02,966 --> 00:05:05,397
beginning was a different observation.

112
00:05:06,227 --> 00:05:08,856
Here we're following a Pacific species that

113
00:05:08,868 --> 00:05:12,258
researchers found carrying tiny lures on the finest

114
00:05:12,270 --> 00:05:13,840
branches of its tentacles.

115
00:05:14,731 --> 00:05:16,181
The whole colony swims.

116
00:05:16,992 --> 00:05:18,880
The invitation to a fish comes from a

117
00:05:18,892 --> 00:05:20,103
much smaller piece.

118
00:05:21,353 --> 00:05:23,906
Follow one tentacle in the enlarged view.

119
00:05:24,797 --> 00:05:26,688
Now take a branch off that branch.

120
00:05:27,519 --> 00:05:29,651
At the end is a lure that can twitch.

121
00:05:30,522 --> 00:05:33,532
A fish approaching from the darkness would encounter

122
00:05:33,544 --> 00:05:36,195
that little moving target before it could work out

123
00:05:36,207 --> 00:05:38,179
the outline of the hunter behind it.

124
00:05:39,069 --> 00:05:41,400
These few inches of screen stand in for a much

125
00:05:41,412 --> 00:05:42,482
smaller structure.

126
00:05:43,693 --> 00:05:45,925
The color is a clue to how the lure works.

127
00:05:46,796 --> 00:05:48,567
Living tissue produces light.

128
00:05:49,358 --> 00:05:51,608
The researchers also found fluorescent

129
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:52,891
material in the lure.

130
00:05:53,802 --> 00:05:56,032
It absorbs light and gives some back at

131
00:05:56,044 --> 00:05:57,195
a different wavelength.

132
00:05:58,006 --> 00:06:01,036
Put those processes together and the mature lure can

133
00:06:01,048 --> 00:06:02,659
send out a reddish signal.

134
00:06:03,470 --> 00:06:06,223
The surrounding colony doesn't have to light up.

135
00:06:07,393 --> 00:06:08,704
Here's a lanternfish,

136
00:06:09,054 --> 00:06:12,204
the kind of small midwater fish that puts the size of

137
00:06:12,216 --> 00:06:13,727
this meal in perspective.

138
00:06:14,678 --> 00:06:15,628
Notice the eye.

139
00:06:16,459 --> 00:06:18,350
Now imagine its view of the lure.

140
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:22,390
Scientists have suggested the twitching tip resembles

141
00:06:22,402 --> 00:06:24,794
a small crustacean a fish might eat.

142
00:06:25,644 --> 00:06:27,756
We can describe the light and the motion.

143
00:06:28,586 --> 00:06:31,218
What the fish takes them to mean is an inference.

144
00:06:32,448 --> 00:06:34,780
That distinction changes how you watch a hunt.

145
00:06:35,670 --> 00:06:38,903
A glowing tip in a frame is evidence for a glowing tip.

146
00:06:39,773 --> 00:06:42,243
A fish found inside a feeding polyp is

147
00:06:42,255 --> 00:06:43,346
evidence for a meal.

148
00:06:44,196 --> 00:06:46,466
The tempting story in between has to

149
00:06:46,478 --> 00:06:47,989
fit both observations.

150
00:06:48,839 --> 00:06:51,349
The tentacle puts stinging cells within reach of a

151
00:06:51,361 --> 00:06:53,793
fish drawn toward the moving light.

152
00:06:55,023 --> 00:06:58,356
Suppose you found only the bell end in a sample jar.

153
00:06:59,207 --> 00:07:01,459
You could work out how the colony swam,

154
00:07:01,770 --> 00:07:04,062
and miss almost everything about what it ate.

155
00:07:04,933 --> 00:07:07,205
Suppose you found only a loose tentacle.

156
00:07:07,996 --> 00:07:09,487
You might see the bright tip,

157
00:07:09,777 --> 00:07:12,630
yet never know where it hung in relation to the mouth.

158
00:07:13,501 --> 00:07:15,572
The arrangement itself is evidence.

159
00:07:16,803 --> 00:07:20,155
This is where a remotely operated vehicle earns its

160
00:07:20,167 --> 00:07:21,358
place in the story.

161
00:07:22,269 --> 00:07:24,360
A tether connects the vehicle to a ship.

162
00:07:25,151 --> 00:07:28,024
The people watching the screens above can stop,

163
00:07:28,294 --> 00:07:30,705
turn, and look along a colony that's still

164
00:07:30,717 --> 00:07:31,988
suspended in water.

165
00:07:32,839 --> 00:07:36,110
They can record which end is pulsing before deciding

166
00:07:36,122 --> 00:07:37,172
where to look next.

167
00:07:38,363 --> 00:07:41,332
Start the camera too close and every ripple looks

168
00:07:41,344 --> 00:07:42,654
like a separate jelly.

169
00:07:43,464 --> 00:07:46,575
Pull all the way back and the finest branches vanish.

170
00:07:47,405 --> 00:07:50,336
The wide view supplies an address for the close view.

171
00:07:51,226 --> 00:07:52,936
First locate the swimming end.

172
00:07:53,786 --> 00:07:55,337
Then follow the shared stem.

173
00:07:56,227 --> 00:07:59,416
Only then does a close pass by a tentacle tell you

174
00:07:59,428 --> 00:08:01,358
which part of the animal you're seeing.

175
00:08:02,568 --> 00:08:04,698
The light used to see the colony changes

176
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:05,761
the picture, too.

177
00:08:06,692 --> 00:08:09,662
Expedition lamps reveal the outline of transparent

178
00:08:09,674 --> 00:08:12,827
tissue as it bends and reflects their light.

179
00:08:13,717 --> 00:08:15,409
A lure making light of its own

180
00:08:15,739 --> 00:08:17,130
asks a different question.

181
00:08:18,001 --> 00:08:20,831
An illuminated portrait and an observation of a

182
00:08:20,843 --> 00:08:22,094
glow in darkness

183
00:08:22,425 --> 00:08:24,955
can describe the same animal while showing

184
00:08:24,967 --> 00:08:26,157
quite different things.

185
00:08:27,448 --> 00:08:29,058
Go back to Puerto Rico.

186
00:08:29,869 --> 00:08:31,639
The photograph hasn't changed.

187
00:08:32,429 --> 00:08:34,500
Try reading it from the tip this time.

188
00:08:35,310 --> 00:08:37,460
There's a region built for pushing water,

189
00:08:37,670 --> 00:08:41,041
then a bend, then structures with a different outline.

190
00:08:41,891 --> 00:08:44,820
You can recognize the division of labor without

191
00:08:44,832 --> 00:08:47,461
assigning this animal the red lures of

192
00:08:47,473 --> 00:08:48,803
its Pacific relative.

193
00:08:50,073 --> 00:08:52,323
So is it one hunter or many animals?

194
00:08:53,234 --> 00:08:56,202
Follow the development and you find individuals

195
00:08:56,214 --> 00:08:57,845
budding from a common beginning.

196
00:08:58,695 --> 00:08:59,705
Follow a turn,

197
00:09:00,035 --> 00:09:03,106
and one set of individuals steers while another drives.

198
00:09:03,976 --> 00:09:04,986
Follow a meal,

199
00:09:05,316 --> 00:09:07,605
and the tentacle and the mouth belong to one

200
00:09:07,617 --> 00:09:08,727
connected system.

201
00:09:09,537 --> 00:09:11,567
Count the workers and you get many.

202
00:09:12,397 --> 00:09:14,728
Watch the hunt and you see one colony.

